WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.520
EVO got its start in the arcades of Southern California, but has since grown to the biggest fighting game

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.960
tournament in the world.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.920
Over its first thirteen years, it went from just a handful of competitors, to over a thousand.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.920
And it's crowned champions, like Alex Valle.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.600
Justin Wong.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.380
And Daigo Umehara

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.700
The tournament survived the dark ages of the mid 2000s,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.960
And exploded in 2009 when Street Fighter IV came out.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.700
But people couldn't even imagine, just how big it was going to get.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.160
EVO began as a way for the American FGC to come together and settle the score,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.220
But eventually,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:59.140
it became an international festival, dedicated to celebrating the FGC, preserving Arcade culture

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.540
and crowning world champions.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.300
And after surviving a dark age and weathering the storm of the mid-2000s, Evo bounced back

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.500
in 2009.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.120
Thanks in part to Street Fighter IV’s arrival.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:22.400
(Casting)

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.400
After winning in 2009, Street Fighter legend Daigo Umehara turned his historic legacy into

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.880
something more modern, more real.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.520
And in 2010, he did it all over again.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.920
The Super Street Fighter IV tournament at Evo 2010 saw nearly double the entrants, but

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.940
all the warriors in the world couldn’t stop Daigo, who brought down Shizza, Mike Ross

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.340
and beat Ricki Ortiz twice to take home his second straight Evo win.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.160
"Daigo Umehara is your Evolution 2010 Champion."

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:53.780
"Wow."

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.220
"He still reigns supreme."

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.080
Meanwhile, the Marvel 2 show was just about over.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:06.450
By the time Justin won in 2010, it seemed like it was done as a tournament game.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.470
Street Fighter IV brought Evo back to life, but fans simply couldn’t believe that Capcom

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.000
could do it again.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.180
That is, not until Marvel vs. Capcom 3.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.560
Just like with Street Fighter IV, Capcom did what many thought was impossible, and gave

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.800
fans a follow up to a legendary, decade-old game.It was an era just before Marvel’s

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.700
movies got huge, an era before the FGC was being integrated into the larger esports ecosystem.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.200
It was basically the only time it could happen.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:50.080
(Casting)

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.470
Viscant took first in 2011, surprising plenty of people who expected one of the old Marvel

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.680
gods to take the tournament, But Justin tailed behind in third place.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.920
"Justin Wong. He's smiling, I know he's gonna take this one inside."

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.060
"He worked really hard."

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.980
In 2012 Justin dropped to a tie for 7th.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.040
as Fchamp took the top spot.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.440
"Champ 2012 Evolution Champion, wow."

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.120
The next year though, Justin got close.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:24.700
He was in the Grand Finals against Flocker, and played an unbelievably close set.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.960
"You know what Flocker won, but I definitely give the MVP to Justin Wong."

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.420
"He had to climb."

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:37.560
And finally, in 2014, Justin reclaimed his throne, with an underdog win against Chris G

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.980
"Justin Wong takes it!"

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.140
"He takes it!"

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.140
Justin’s win, and of course, his now iconic

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.900
pop-off, all-but ended an era of Marvel at Evo.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.560
Sure, the game continued to appear at the tournament for three more years, but things

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.020
felt like they were slowing down.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.720
If only a little.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.980
KaneBlueRiver took an underdog victory in 2015

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.120
"Keep in mind, this is the first tournament that KBR has ever won."

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.140
"What a tournament for it to be."

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.900
ChrisG won in 2016 before a frightening visit from the spirit of Marvel,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.100
"Chris G"

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:23.240
And in 2017, RyanLV, the hometown hero, won what looks to be the last official Marvel

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.440
vs Capcom 3 tournament Evo will ever see.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:37.240
(Casting)

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.000
While Marvel 3’s history at Evo was explosive, the franchise left the event

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.380
with more of a whimper.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.720
Marvel vs. Capcom Infinite was left off the official roster in 2018, due to weak sales

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.120
and Evo not believing there were enough players to hold a tournament.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.080
And while Marvel will be at the event as a side tournament, the biggest fighting game

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.540
tournament in the world looks like it’s leaving one of the games that built it behind.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.140
But in Marvel’s glory days at Evo, it created some of the tournament’s hypest moments.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.800
In 2011, Justin Wong updated his legendary 2007 comeback with a new one in Marvel 3.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:23.700
(Casting)

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.380
And eight-year-old Noah Solis shocked the world with his own incredible Marvel play.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.460
"Great job to Noah, came out here."

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.340
"I mean how do you not give that kid all the respect in the world."

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.980
"Top 48 out of like 1500 people for an eight year old kid."

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.840
In Street Fighter that year, Saudi Arabian player Latif went deeper in Street Fighter

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.000
IV than anyone could have expected.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.820
Latif has eliminated Daigo 'The Beast' Umehara."

00:05:54.040 --> 00:06:00.000
The next year, 2012 belonged to international competitors, as Infiltration won Street Fighter IV.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.720
(Casting)

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:12.200
And Korea’s MadKoF dueled Mexico’s Bala for the King of Fighters XIII title.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:27.120
(Casting)

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:33.480
2013 brought controversy, but it also brought back a game that would change Evo forever.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.889
Evo’s organizers announced the seven tournament games for 2013 in January, but also announced

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:41.540
a charity drive to add an eighth game to the lineup.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.240
The rules were simple.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.860
Whichever community raised the most money for breast cancer research got their game at Evo.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.140
There were over a dozen games vying for the last slot at Evo, but the real competition

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:59.040
was between indie darling Skullgirls and Melee, which hadn’t been at Evo since 2007.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:05.960
(Casting)

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.500
Both games broke five digit donations, but by February, Melee came out on top, raising

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:22.180
$94,683 for breast cancer research out  
of the total $225,744 raised by the FGC at large.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.700
Skullgirls has never been an official Evo game.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:29.080
Melee, on the other hand, would go on to flourish at Evo.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.300
But not before some hiccups.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:36.520
The day before Evo 2013, Nintendo barred the tournament organizers from not only streaming

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.800
the Melee tournament, but holding it.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:45.340
"Nintendo has taken it away from the fans. Literally saying that, Evo can hold the tournament, but you can't

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:50.820
actually host the games. This is freaking ridiculous you can't even. They can't stream now."

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:57.580
"It's their IP, they can do what they want. And they didn't present us with any options to keep it open

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.680
they were just, "Hey we want to shut you down."

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.860
Nintendo doubled back on their decision later that day.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:07.660
Evo didn’t even do anything, it was the power of the fans who clamored for their game

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:11.800
to be on fighting games’ most important, most prestigious stage.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:17.120
"We wanted to be recognized as a fighting game, first of all. And we also wanted to be recognized as an esport

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:21.080
So it was really really important for us to get into Evo so that we can have that recognition."

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.760
The next year, Smash was at Evo, and Nintendo sponsored the tournament.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:33.300
"I wanted to officially wish you luck in the final stages of the Super Smash Bros. Melee tournament at EVO 2014."

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:38.400
Smash has gone on to become one of Evo’s biggest games, with both Melee and Super Smash

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.540
Bros. for Wii U right behind Street Fighter as the event’s largest tournaments.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:44.040
Year after year.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:55.960
Melee went from 692 players in 2013 to 970 the next year, then 1,873 in 2015, and 2,373

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.220
in 2016.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:03.060
Smash 4, which joined Evo in 2015, debuted with 1,929 players.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.980
The next year, it was at 2,656.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:13.960
In fact Evo 2015 and 2016 are the four biggest Smash tournaments of all time .

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:19.940
Smash cemented its place at Evo with growing attendance numbers year over year, but things

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.220
haven’t been easy.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:25.720
Tensions between the fighting game and Smash communities have led to plenty of disagreements

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.260
between the two scenes.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.620
The most notable example?

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.200
Smash’s struggle for the Sunday stage.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.760
"I think Melee really really needs to, you know calm down with that and they really need to just

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:45.600
accept what's given to us, like don't ask for more. We're already at Evo, don't be like spoiled.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.720
I think the Smash community is like really spoiled."

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:54.240
Melee and Smash 4 traded their spots in Sunday’s stage show back and forth, with the 2017 Smash

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.400
4 Top 8 even being televised on Disney XD.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:04.840
"The 2017 EVO World Championship Super Smash Bros. Live event Sunday 6 eastern 3 pacific on DXP."

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:09.940
Meanwhile, certain fighting game fans often wondered why Smash got to have two spots at

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:13.180
Evo, while series like , say, Street Fighter, only got one.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:19.720
But at this point, there’s no getting Smash out of Evo, and likely, no getting Evo out of Smash.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:32.440
(Casting)

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.280
Evo’s spotlight shines bright, and like it brought Smash to new relevance outside of

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.740
just the Smash community, plenty of games have made legends at Evo.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:49.520
KoF had yet another incredible Grand Finals in 2013, when Reynald and Woo battled for the fate of the game.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:55.140
(Casting)

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.400
"It was like so hype and energetic, I remember Reynald putting on the USA shades.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.780
You know you have the shades with the American flag on there after winning

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:09.640
he was waving the American flag and everything like that. That was also probably one of the greatest

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.420
Evo Top 8 moments as well."

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.400
While Xian and Tokido’s SFIV grand finals set was somewhat overshadowed by Infiltration’s

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.580
Hakan pick in the Losers’ semis.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:24.960
2014 brought Axe’s now-iconic 4-stock on Silent Wolf,

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.500
"Destructiooooooon!

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:32.400
an unbelievable run from Blazblue player Galileo,

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:44.840
(Casting)

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:48.640
and the biggest win the EU FGC had ever seen.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:57.420
(Casting)

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.500
"To be honest with you I think what he accomplished is probably one of the greatest accomplishments

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.540
of fighting games.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:07.740
You know, history. To be able to go through the losers bracket and take out so many killers.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:13.640
And end up winning Evo against a guy like Bonchan. You know who at the time felt like he was

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:19.520
the shoe-in to win Evo that year. For me it was a really important moment.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:26.040
And really kind of opened the door a lot of- for the world wide scene of Street Fighter."

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.360
Evo has changed over the years though.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:36.300
It’s not just the games that are played or the size of the venue, it’s the atmosphere.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:41.320
While Evo got its start as a way to preserve arcade culture, it eventually became the source

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:42.420
of a new one.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:43.680
Tournament culture.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:49.060
Evo was the first massive fighting game tournament, and while we have plenty of majors all over

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:51.340
the world now, Evo was first.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.020
And that means something.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:58.880
"The community around fighting games are amazing like, so close knit and very humble and very like

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:01.240
supportive of each other, it's a really great experience."

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:06.080
Almost everyone at Evo is there to play, and even when they’re out, they’re playing

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:11.600
casual matches, meeting up with old friends and celebrating their love of fighting games together.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:14.720
Evo is a mecca for fighting game culture.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:20.580
Nearly every fighting game fan in the world wants to go, at least once, to know what it’s like

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.680
to be in that room.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:27.140
To watch that stage, to get to play with the best, the worst and everyone else.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:32.160
Evo is a celebration of the fighting game community, but it takes an incredible group

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.740
of people to make that celebration worth it, year after year.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.520
2015 might be best known for the viral “What are you standing up for” clip.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.740
"What are you standing up for?"

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:45.580
"Why?"

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:47.100
"Oh my god!"

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:53.120
"Woshige thought it was over. And stood up, and Ogawa gave him no remorse."

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:56.840
"That is exactly what happens. Do not celebrate pop off!"

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:58.620
"Oh my god!"

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:02.640
But the play in other tournaments that year was just as good.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:07.040
ZeRo won Evo’s first Smash 4 tournament without dropping a single game.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:26.320
(Casting)

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.120
And in the Street Fighter IV grand finals, the unthinkable happened.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:35.640
(Casting)

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.400
"No. No. No James, not like this. Not like this."

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:47.440
Momochi’s stick may have broken mid-set, but he soldiered through and won the tournament.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:15:02.640
(Casting)

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:07.440
Perhaps the most notable shift from the underground Evos of yesteryear to the glitz and glamor

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:12.720
of the Mandalay Bay stage broadcast on ESPN is the amount of developer support the tournament

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.700
has seen in recent years.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:19.860
Capcom first kicked money into the Evo Street Fighter prize pool in 2006, but their contribution

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.600
has grown over the years.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:25.920
These days their contributions include more money, status as the biggest tournament on

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.440
the Capcom Pro Tour and even new character announcements.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:38.000
Ultra Street Fighter IV was even announced at Evo 2013, with a trailer packed with Evo footage.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:46.380
But Capcom isn’t the only company to turn around and support Evo and the FGC at large

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.400
thanks to the tournament’s incredible growth.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:54.240
Nintendo went from blocking Smash tournaments in 2013 to sponsoring Evo every year since.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:59.780
Namco Bandai announced Tekken 7 at Evo 2014, and have been announcing new characters at

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:04.860
Evo ever since, blowing fans away with their Geese Howard announcement in 2017.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:14.280
And Arc System Works has become one of Evo’s biggest supporters, announcing new Guilty

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:19.740
Gear and Dragon Ball FighterZ characters, along with BlazBlue Cross Tag Battle at Evo 2017.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:21.700
"Yes!"

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:27.980
And at launch, Cross Tag Battle was even branded with the Evo logo on the shrink wrap, telling

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.640
potential buyers that it  was an Evo game.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:37.520
But there is some push back. Cross Tag Battle, was added to EVO 2018's lineup months before the game

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:38.460
was launched.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.020
Leading to some controversy.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.680
But, either through luck or intuition.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:48.160
Evo got it right, and Cross Tag Battle was popular.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:52.520
Or maybe its presence at Evo made people take an interest in the game.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:57.740
Evo was built from the ground up by fighting game fans, in a time when support like this

00:16:57.740 --> 00:16:59.180
was unheard of.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:04.560
Now, companies are flocking to Evo because it’s the best way to reach the FGC, the

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.600
core audience they seemed to abandon for years.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:12.880
"You may call me, G."

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:18.520
And at this point, it’s possible that these companies need Evo more than Evo needs them.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:23.140
In 2016, Evo exploded again.

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:28.180
The launch of Street Fighter V earlier that year brought fans to Evo in droves, with more

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:31.100
than 5,000 people in one bracket.

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:36.160
No other fighting game tournament has even come close since, not even the next Evo.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:42.300
2017 was smaller than 2016, but only because the SFV explosion was unprecedented.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:45.780
It was bigger than 2009, bigger than anything.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:50.540
And that growth has also led to other fighting games finding success at Evo.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:54.540
(Casting)

00:17:54.560 --> 00:18:00.000
Tekken joined the 1,000 club in 2017 and even smaller games, like Guilty Gear and Killer

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.480
Instinct have seen strong turnouts at Evo.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:09.980
(Casting)

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:15.780
Evo itself has even grown to include Evo Japan, a 2018 tournament that marked Evo’s first

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:17.440
time outside North America.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:23.260
Evo 2016 was bigger than ever, which meant the moments were even more explosive.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:29.280
Hungrybox shocked the world with an incredible reset and victory over Melee defending champion, Armada.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:59.000
(Casting)

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:04.180
And LI Joe fought for America in Street Fighter V, all the way to Top 8.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:19.860
(Casting)

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:25.900
And while Evo 2017 may have had smaller attendance numbers, the tournament still saw some incredible runs.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:30.440
JDCR and Saint duked it out for Tekken 7’s first place trophy.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:37.740
(Casting)

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:43.680
Salem emerged triumphant over ZeRo after a grueling, 10-game Smash 4 Grand Finals.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:56.240
(Casting)

00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:01.440
And Tokido made one of the greatest Top 8 runs of all time at Evo, ending it with a

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.160
message of love for the FGC.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:13.120
"Just one thing I want to say. Fighting game is, something so great."

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:23.240
The next year, Tokido made it back to grand finals, but Street Fighter crowned an unexpexted champion.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:35.800
"Problem-X has taken Evolution 2018. And all of UK is elated. They have run up there to swarm him,

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:39.760
Tokido, has been sent home in second place."

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:44.740
And just before that, the Dragonball FighterZ Grand Finals between Sonic Fox and Go1,

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:47.600
was the most viewed set in EVO history.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:21:01.800
(Casting)

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:15.120
There is nothing in the world, quite like Evo.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:19.460
No esports tournament in the world has the history, the legacy.

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:25.600
No tournament has the sheer number of competitors, all of whom get that same shot at the top.

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:31.760
No tournament brings the hype, the joy, the sadness and the incredible feeling of victory

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:36.820
that comes with emerging from hundreds, from thousands of competitors as the champion.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:39.940
But there’s more to Evo than just the winner.

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:45.960
Evo was built by the FGC, and each year is made not just by just the people behind the

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.080
scenes, working their asses off.

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:54.320
It’s made by every competitor, every spectator, every stream monster who contributes to the experience.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.380
Evo is about the FGC.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:22:01.400
It’s a celebration of a community that’s been through everything.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.960
It’s the greatest tournament in the gaming world,

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:12.080
"And that is the best way to put it, Evo is love, the people come back this event is unlike anything else.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:15.300
In the entire video game world."

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:18.200
and no matter what happens, it only

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:20.540
gets better every year.

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:16.160
"Hadouken!"

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:21.340
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

